  "dom_id": "step-q62825",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "A tank has an inlet and an outlet. When the inlet is turned on, the rate at which water flows into the tank governed by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=9{t}^{2}+18,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nWhen the outlet is switched on, water leaves the tank through it at a rate of $14$ liters per minute.\n\nThe inlet is switched on, and the water level increases to $120$ liters, at which point the outlet is also switched on. How much water is in the tank $10$ minutes after switching on the outlet, given that the outlet is switched on at $t=0?$",
  "worked_solution": "Water enters the tank at a rate of $9{t}^{2}+18$ liters per minute and leaves at a rate of $14$ liters per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change of volume** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+18-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total volume** of water in the tank after $t$ minutes (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the volume of water after $10$ minutes, we substitute $t=10$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(10) & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{10}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{[3{x}^{3}+4x]|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =120+[(3(10)^{3}+4(10))-(0)] \\\\  & =120+3040 \\\\  & =3\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$",
